Trust Policy:
  this is resource-base-polocy for IAM role.It define what principle can assume this role.
  The principals that you can specify in the trust policy include  users, roles, accounts, and services.
  Principles in Trust policy:-
    1. Any AWS service in same account
          "Principal": {
              "Service": ["ec2.amazonaws.com"]
          }
   2. Other AWS account:-
        "Principal": {"AWS": "34567876545"}  OR
        "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:root  "}  
  
    3. Any IAM user of same or other AWS account:-
        "Principal": {
            "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::34567876545:user/USERNAME"
        },  
        --> This give permission to assume this role by any role/user of aws account '34567876545'. this is i trust your whole account.
    4. Any IAM role same or other AWS account:-
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::34567876545:role/ROLE_NAME"
      },
    5. Specific service of same AWS account:-
        "Principal": {
          "Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com"
        },
    6. Direcly it cannnot be attached to any AWS service of other AWs account.
        but it can be attached to a Role[from other AWS account] that is attached to that external AWS service. 
             "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<vendor_account>:role_or_user/<role_or_user_in _vendor_acc_of_lambda"},
             "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "your-unique-external-id"}}}
    7. Web identity provider like:-
        AWs Cognito, Facebook, Google etc.
    8. SAML 2.0 corporrte user directory


External Id:-
  This is useful in case of role is assumed by another aws account. External id gives extra layer of protection. 
  You set the externalId in trust policy and the clientee aws account must have to send this external Id in the STS:AssumeRole api call.
  Ex Scenario:-
    A new relic company can provide the service to many AWS account including one you own.
    You add that newrelic aws account in your trust policy principle. Now new relic account can assume role of you aws account.
    What if a malicious party somhow know the arn of role of home aws account and decive "new_relic" to assume same role on behalf of him.
    but if external id is set at both side, hacker must also know that id too.
    Trust policy of my aws account:-
    {
        "Principal": {"AWS": "New relic AWS account ID"},
        "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "Unique ID of New relic"}}
    }
    Limitation of External Id:-
      If external id chech is there in  trust policy then, "swich Role" feture for that role wil not work. The good practine is that if cross accout sharing is  done 
      withine the org then do not use external id bot for third party aws account do use it.

